# Cauldron2D art in a notebook

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:cauldron_2d_easel_kino, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## What this notebook shows

Cauldron2D draws a game from an **atlas**: a catalogue of tiles, each a small picture
with a name. The pictures live in an art directory as `.pic` files — text, with a palette
and a grid of letters — or as `.png` files. This notebook opens the editor in a cell,
makes a static tile and an animated one with `Cauldron2D.Easel`, looks at them with
`Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino`, derives more art from them with `Linocut`, and installs the
directory as an atlas the way a game does.

Everything here is a plain function on a value; the editors
(`mix cauldron.easel` in a terminal, `Cauldron2D.Easel.Web` in a browser, the cell below)
call the same functions.

## The editor in a cell

`Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.Editor.new/2` is the atlas editor as a cell. Paint with the
pointer; the buttons are the tools, the palette, the frames and the history. A new
picture starts with no colours: choose one in the colour input and press **add colour**,
and the brush paints with it. With more than one frame, **onion** shows the previous frame
faintly under the current one and **play** runs them at the rate set.

```elixir
alias Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.Editor

editor = Editor.new(Cauldron2D.Easel.new(16, 16))
```

The picture as it stands is a value any later cell can use — evaluate this after
painting:

```elixir
editor
|> Editor.easel()
|> Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(scale: 8)
```

Opened on a `.pic` or `.png` path, **save** writes the file back; with `atlas:` and
`tile:` it then installs the directory as that atlas, so a game running in this notebook
picks the change up at once. The art directory this notebook makes is opened this way
further down.

## A picture as text

A `.pic` is a palette — one character and a colour a line — a blank line, and the
picture. `.` is transparent.

```elixir
pic = """
W #f0f0f8
G #4a4a60
B #3b5bdb

....WWWWWWWW....
...WGGGGGGGGW...
..WGBBGGGGBBGW..
..WGBBGGGGBBGW..
..WGGGGGGGGGGW..
..WGGGGGGGGGGW..
..WGGBGGGGBGGW..
..WGGGBBBBGGGW..
...WGGGGGGGGW...
....WWWWWWWW....
................
................
................
................
................
................
"""

{:ok, face} = Cauldron2D.Easel.from_pic(pic)
Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(face, scale: 12)
```

## Drawing with functions

The same picture can be made from a blank easel. Every operation returns a new easel,
and `undo/1` walks back through them.

```elixir
alias Cauldron2D.Easel

drawn =
  Easel.new(16, 16, palette: [{"W", "#f0f0f8"}, {"G", "#4a4a60"}, {"B", "#3b5bdb"}])
  |> Easel.rect({4, 0}, {11, 9}, "W")
  |> Easel.fill({7, 5}, "G")
  |> Easel.rect({3, 2}, {4, 3}, "B", fill: true)
  |> Easel.rect({11, 2}, {12, 3}, "B", fill: true)
  |> Easel.line({4, 7}, {11, 7}, "B")

Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(drawn, scale: 12)
```

```elixir
drawn |> Easel.undo() |> Easel.undo() |> Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(scale: 12)
```

## An animated picture

Frames are the same picture repeated with a change; `.pic` keeps them separated by a
`---` line, and the easel plays them at its rate.

```elixir
flame =
  Easel.new(16, 16, palette: [{"R", "#ff4a1a"}, {"Y", "#ffd23c"}, {"D", "#7a1a00"}], fps: 6)
  |> Easel.rect({6, 8}, {9, 15}, "R", fill: true)
  |> Easel.rect({7, 11}, {8, 15}, "Y", fill: true)
  |> Easel.duplicate_frame()
  |> Easel.shift(0, -2)
  |> Easel.paint({5, 9}, "D")
  |> Easel.paint({10, 9}, "D")
  |> Easel.duplicate_frame()
  |> Easel.shift(0, -1)
  |> Easel.flip(:horizontal)

Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.animate(flame, scale: 12)
```

The frames side by side, as a `.png` of the art would hold them:

```elixir
Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(flame, scale: 6)
```

## An art directory

A game keeps its pictures in a directory; here a temporary one. `Cauldron2D.Easel.Project`
saves each easel as `<name>.pic` and keeps the glyph and colour the text renderer draws
for each art in `atlas.txt` beside them.

```elixir
alias Cauldron2D.Easel.Project

dir = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "easel_notebook")
File.mkdir_p!(dir)
project = Project.open(dir, tile: 16)

{:ok, project} = Project.save(project, :face, face)
{:ok, project} = Project.save(project, :flame, flame)
{:ok, project} = Project.put_entry(project, :face, glyph: "@ ", color: {240, 240, 248})
{:ok, project} = Project.put_entry(project, :flame, glyph: "^ ", color: {255, 74, 26})

Project.arts(project)
```

```elixir
File.read!(Path.join(dir, "atlas.txt"))
```

The whole directory as one sheet — what a browser or a window is sent:

```elixir
Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.sheet(dir, tile: 16, scale: 4)
```

The flame opened in the editor from its file. **save** writes `flame.pic` back and
installs the directory as the `:notebook` atlas; the sheet cell above and the surface cell
at the end show the change once re-evaluated.

```elixir
Editor.new(Path.join(dir, "flame.pic"), atlas: :notebook, tile: 16)
```

## One drawing, many views

A picture's keys mean something — `W` the face, `B` the eyes — so a second view is a line
of operations on the keys rather than a second drawing: `Cauldron2D.Easel.derive/3` takes
the operations `Linocut.Derive` reads (`colour`, `swap`, `flip`, `turn`, `shift`, `frame`,
`reverse`, `over`) and gives a new easel.

```elixir
{:ok, green_eyed} = Easel.derive(face, "colour B #20c040; flip h")
Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(green_eyed, scale: 12)
```

`over` lays another picture on, its transparent keys letting the face through; the
pictures it names come from a function.

```elixir
hat = """
K #202020

..KKKKKKKKKKKK..
...KKKKKKKKKK...
....KKKKKKKK....
................
................
................
................
................
................
................
................
................
................
................
................
................
"""

{:ok, hatted} = Easel.derive(face, "over hat 0 -1", fn "hat" -> {:ok, hat}; _ -> :error end)
Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(hatted, scale: 12)
```

In an art directory the same line lives in `atlas.txt`, so the atlas builds the derived art
from the drawn one every time it loads — change the drawing and every view follows:

```elixir
{:ok, project} = Project.derive(project, :face_green, "from face; colour B #20c040")
{:ok, project} = Project.derive(project, :face_left, "from face_green; flip h")
Project.arts(project) |> Enum.map(&{&1.name, Map.get(&1, :derived, Map.get(&1, :file))})
```

```elixir
Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.sheet(dir, tile: 16, scale: 4)
```

## Deriving art with Linocut

`Linocut` is what the engine draws pictures with: headings of a shape, a recoloured
sprite, a scaled or turned one. Its results are rasters, so `image/2` shows them too.

```elixir
ship = Linocut.headings([{15, 0}, {-8, 8}, {-8, -8}], 16, size: {16, 16}, fill: :purple)

ship
|> Linocut.Preview.sheet(columns: 8, gap: 1)
|> Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(scale: 4)
```

```elixir
face
|> Easel.raster(0)
|> Linocut.recolour(%{{59, 91, 219, 255} => {230, 60, 60, 255}})
|> Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(scale: 12)
```

## Installing the atlas

This is what a game does at start, and what the editors do on every save with an atlas
named: the directory becomes a `Cauldron2D.Atlas`, glyphs and rates from the manifest.

```elixir
{:ok, :notebook} = Project.install(project, :notebook)
atlas = Cauldron2D.Atlas.fetch(:notebook)

for art <- Cauldron2D.Atlas.arts(atlas) do
  entry = Cauldron2D.Atlas.entry(atlas, art)
  {art, entry.glyph, length(entry.frames), entry.fps}
end
```

A surface composed from that atlas, as the terminal would draw it in pixels — a row of
faces with the flame's second frame over one of them:

```elixir
rows = [[{:face, [], nil}, {:face, [:flame], nil}, {:face, [], nil}]]

Cauldron2D.Surface.compose(:notebook, rows, [], at: 340)
|> Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.image(scale: 6)
```

`at:` is the time in milliseconds the animated art's frame is chosen for; try `0`, `170`
and `340` to see the flame's three frames.

## Parallax layers

A set of `Cauldron2D.Parallax` layers can be looked at before any game exists:
`Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.parallax/3` runs a camera across them and shows the pictures in
turn, which is the only way to judge a factor.

Four depths over the notebook's own atlas — a pinned back wall, two scattered middles and
a scatter in front that streams past faster than the ground:

```elixir
alias Cauldron2D.Parallax

layers = [
  Parallax.layer(
    name: :wall,
    cell: fn _ -> {:face, [], nil} end,
    factor: 0.0,
    void: :opaque
  ),
  Parallax.layer(
    name: :far,
    cell: Parallax.scatter(seed: 11, density: 0.08, arts: [:flame]),
    factor: {0.25, 1.0}
  ),
  Parallax.layer(
    name: :near,
    cell: Parallax.scatter(seed: 22, density: 0.05, arts: [:face]),
    factor: {0.7, 1.0}
  ),
  Parallax.layer(
    name: :rush,
    cell: Parallax.scatter(seed: 33, density: 0.02, arts: [:flame]),
    factor: {1.6, 1.0},
    front: true
  )
]

Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.parallax(:notebook, layers,
  focus: {0.0, 0.0},
  size: {20, 10},
  travel: {40.0, 0.0},
  frames: 80,
  zoom: 3
)
```

Change a `factor` and run the cell again: `0.0` pins a layer to the pane, `1.0` moves it
with the world, and anything above `1.0` passes in front of it. `Parallax.scatter/1`
places art by hashing the tile's own coordinate, so a layer goes on forever without
holding anything, and the same seed always lays it out the same way.

A layer that is one picture rather than a grid of tiles uses `Parallax.panorama/1`,
which copies the picture across the pane instead of blending it:

```elixir
sky =
  Parallax.panorama(
    name: :sky,
    art: :face,
    size: {4, 4},
    wrap: :both,
    factor: 0.1,
    void: :opaque
  )

Cauldron2D.Easel.Kino.parallax(:notebook, [sky | tl(layers)],
  focus: {0.0, 0.0},
  size: {20, 10},
  travel: {40.0, 0.0},
  frames: 80,
  zoom: 3
)
```
